WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.733
Currents and Critters is a card game about building an ocean.

00:00:04.033 --> 00:00:06.450
Two to eight players. Here is how it works.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.917
Your job is to create the highest scoring marine ecosystem.

00:00:11.217 --> 00:00:16.433
Oceans are your foundation. Animals attach to them, and every animal adds points.

00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:19.767
Build it before somebody reveals the end game card.

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:24.300
Setup is quick. Take the end game card out, and shuffle the rest.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:30.050
Deal eight cards to each player. No ocean in your hand? You can discard all eight and redraw.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:36.183
Now hide the end game card. Shuffle it into the bottom fifteen, and put that stack back under the deck.

00:00:36.483 --> 00:00:39.517
Leave room for the draw pile, the Pool, and a graveyard.

00:00:39.817 --> 00:00:44.500
Whoever has visited an ocean most recently goes first, and play moves clockwise.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.333
So here is what you are looking at.

00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:53.633
Your oceans go down in front of you, in a row, left to right. Once an ocean is down, it never moves.

00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:58.600
And your hand. Eight cards to start, and never more than ten at the end of a turn.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.300
Every card tells you five things.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.850
Top left, the cost, in sand dollars.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:05.433
Top right, its symbol.

00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:07.367
Its name, and its species.

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:10.483
And along the bottom, what it is worth, and what it does.

00:01:11.383 --> 00:01:13.917
On your turn you choose one thing, and only one.

00:01:14.217 --> 00:01:17.900
Draw two cards. From the draw pile, from the Pool, or one of each.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:23.367
Play one card. Lay down a new ocean, or attach an animal to one you already have.

00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:26.317
You pay for it by discarding cards to the Pool.

00:01:26.617 --> 00:01:31.000
Or move one animal to a different ocean. It keeps the same orientation.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:35.083
Finish with more than ten cards, and you discard back down to ten.

00:01:35.983 --> 00:01:40.350
Every animal attaches to an ocean, and each ocean has four spaces.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.433
The top is the surface. Birds and baitfish.

00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:45.400
Left and right take the swimmers.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.583
The bottom is the ocean floor.

00:01:47.883 --> 00:01:49.233
One card per space.

00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:57.100
With one exception. Lobsters stack with lobsters, and yellowfin tuna stack with yellowfin tuna, all in the same space.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.333
The sand dollars are the price. One sand dollar, one card out of your hand and into the Pool.

00:02:03.633 --> 00:02:06.200
Any card can pay. It does not have to match.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.967
But match the symbol when you pay, and the card's star switches on.

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:13.083
The puffin's star hands you another play, right away.

00:02:13.383 --> 00:02:17.550
No matching symbol? You still play the card. You just do not get the star.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.117
Some stars play a second card for free.

00:02:20.417 --> 00:02:25.250
The bottlenose dolphin plays a free mammal, so a narwhal goes down in the same turn.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:30.617
Both are left cards, so the narwhal needs a second ocean with its left space still open.

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:36.467
And a free animal always comes from your hand. Never the Pool, the deck, or the discard pile.

00:02:37.367 --> 00:02:39.117
Abilities come in three shapes.

00:02:39.417 --> 00:02:44.167
Flat. A set number, every time. The puffin is plus three, whatever else you own.

00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:50.900
Per type. It counts a kind of card. Two points for every bird you control, so it grows as your board grows.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:57.317
Or a threshold. A chart that jumps. One mantis shrimp is five, two are fifteen, three are thirty.

00:02:57.617 --> 00:03:01.317
And a chart is read once for the whole set. Never once per card.

00:03:02.217 --> 00:03:05.000
Everything you discard lands in the Pool, face up.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:09.017
Anyone can draw from it on their turn, so watch what you throw away.

00:03:09.317 --> 00:03:15.367
And when the Pool hits ten cards, the whole thing goes face down into the graveyard, and the Pool starts again.

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:19.417
Sooner or later, somebody turns over the end game card.

00:03:19.717 --> 00:03:24.017
It is hiding in the bottom fifteen, so nobody knows the turn it arrives on.

00:03:24.317 --> 00:03:28.183
If you drew it part way through a draw, finish the draw, and finish your turn.

00:03:28.483 --> 00:03:31.800
Then every player takes one final turn. Including you.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.600
Then everybody scores.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.733
Count the bottoms, the tops, the sides, and then the oceans.

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:41.667
A lobster is four. Staghorn coral scores three per coral.

00:03:41.967 --> 00:03:44.133
The puffin, three. The pelican, four.

00:03:44.433 --> 00:03:48.117
The penguin is two per bird, and there are three birds here. Six.

00:03:48.417 --> 00:03:52.583
The blue marlin is nine, because it shares its ocean with a mah-hee mah-hee.

00:03:52.883 --> 00:03:56.067
And three coral reefs together score nine on the reef chart.

00:03:56.367 --> 00:03:58.900
Thirty nine points. Highest score wins.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.050
Currents and Critters.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.950
Protect the ocean. Change the tide. From wings to waves.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:08.983
Five percent of every purchase supports ocean conservation.

00:04:09.283 --> 00:04:11.750
Learn more at currents and critters dot com.
